questions.question:
How many of the following relations are equivalence relations?
(i) Set of all triangles in a plane, $T_1$ R $T_2$ if $T_1$ is congruent to $T_2$
(ii) Set of all triangles, $T_1$ R $T_2$ if $T_1$ is similar to $T_2$
(iii) Set of all lines in a plane, $L_1$ R $L_2$ if $L_1$ is perpendicular to $L_2$
(iv) R in the set of real numbers, R = {(a, b) : a ≤ b}
(v) R in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, R = {(a, b) : |a − b| is even}
(vi) R in Z, R = {(a, b) : 4 divides a − b}

Let A = {1, 2, 3, 4} and B = {p, q, r}. How many of the following relations are functions from A to B?
(i) {(1, p), (2, q), (3, r), (4, p)}
(ii) {(1, p), (2, p), (3, p), (4, p)}
(iii) {(1, p), (2, q), (3, r)}
(iv) {(1, p), (1, q), (2, r), (3, p), (4, q)}
(v) {(1, r), (2, q), (3, q), (4, r)}

Let A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {a, b}. How many of the following relations from A to B are functions?
(i) {(1, a), (2, a), (3, a)}
(ii) {(1, a), (2, b)}
(iii) {(1, a), (1, b), (2, a), (3, b)}
(iv) {(1, b), (2, a), (3, b)}

questions.explanation:
Each equivalence relation on a set corresponds to exactly one partition of the set into equivalence classes.
The relation contains $(1, 2)$, so 1 and 2 must lie in the same class. Treat them as a single block P.
We now count the partitions of the three objects $\{P, 3, 4\}$:
- all separate;
- P with 3;
- P with 4;
- 3 with 4;
- all together.
That gives 5 partitions, so there are 5 such equivalence relations.
Answer: (3)
